<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nahdlatul Ulama</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1926">1926</a>–</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as NU</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nahdlatul <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">Ulama</a> (NU) is an Indonesian Islamic organisation founded in 1926 by Hasyim Asy'ari and other religious scholars of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>. It defends traditional <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sunni-islam">Sunni</a> scholarship and local religious practice, and is rooted in the pesantren boarding schools of rural <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. With tens of millions of followers it is often described as the largest Muslim organisation in the world, and its former chairman Abdurrahman Wahid became president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in 1999.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nahdlatul-ulama.jpg" alt="Nahdlatul Ulama" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Nahdlatul Ulama · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nahdlatul_Ulama_Logo.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The name means Revival of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulama">Ulama</a>. The organisation was founded by religious scholars and merchants to defend traditionalist practice, following the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shafii-school">Shafi'i school</a> of law, and its members' economic interests. It set itself against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/modernism">modernism</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/muhammadiyah">Muhammadiyah</a> and PERSIS and against the Salafi Al-Irshad, which rejected local customs shaped by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>'s Hindu and Buddhist past. In theology NU follows the Ash'ari and Maturidi schools, in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sufism">Sufism</a> the path of al-Ghazali and al-Junayd. Its base remained <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-java">East Java</a>, and by 1928 it preached in Javanese as well as Arabic.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Under the Japanese occupation NU joined the Masyumi council, led in practice by Hasyim's son Wahid Hasyim. On 22 October 1945 it declared the fight against the returning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> a holy war binding on every Muslim, and NU led <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/guerrilla-warfare">guerrilla</a> groups such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hezbollah">Hizbullah</a>. After independence it was part of the Masyumi party but left in 1952 and stood alone in 1955, coming third with 18.4 per cent of the vote, almost all of it from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/java">Java</a>. In the 1950s it still sought an Islamic state, and it competed with the Communist Party for the support of the poor. In 1965 it sided with the army under General <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a> and was heavily involved in the mass killings of Indonesian communists.</p><p>Forced into the United Development Party in 1973, NU withdrew in 1984 under Abdurrahman Wahid, known as Gus Dur, who accepted the state ideology <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pancasila">Pancasila</a> as a noble compromise and later apologised for NU's part in the killings of 1965. After <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suharto">Suharto</a>'s fall he founded the National Awakening Party in 1998 and was elected president of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/indonesia">Indonesia</a> in 1999, but was deposed two years later. At its 2010 conference NU barred its officials from holding political posts.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>NU describes its purpose as spreading Islamic teaching. It runs a network of 6,830 pesantren boarding schools and owns 44 universities, and it also funds hospitals and social services. Its highest body is the Syuriah, or Supreme Council, above the Tanfidziyah, the Executive Council, with regional boards reaching down to village branches. In 2014 it launched a campaign against extremism, and in 2019 it called for an end to the term kafir for non-Muslims, while its leaders have also issued a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fatwa">fatwa</a> in favour of female genital mutilation.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nahdlatul Ulama” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
